From 3028fa6354366c041475888be5c96d65affbb44c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: taengu <7606218+taengu@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 10:48:44 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Capture: read the server's bytes in TCP order, and read past
 a hole cleanly

Segments were read in arrival order and their sequence numbers never
looked at. When one never reached the capture (a Wi-Fi player's zone
loads, 2026-10-27), the bytes after it were taken for a frame length:
the walk read mid-packet bytes as lengths for minutes, the zone's spawn
bundle was swallowed, and Scholar Aulla's fight was never shown,
Bloodfang Pnyn's HP was misread (59,408 for 160 million) and his bar
stayed empty.

tcp_order puts late segments back in place, reads retransmitted bytes
once, and reports a hole once it has waited 250 ms (or 256 KB) for its
bytes. The assembler then drops the frame the hole cut and starts again
at the next whole frame (framing::resync_start: frames that walk to the
end, opening with the opcodes 99.3% of packets have). Replayed with the
hole marked, Aulla's spawn is read (2100988), and without it is not.
The packet log and the share ring now keep the ordered bytes. A payload
without a sequence number (a replay's) is read as before.
